# 👀 Step 1 — Explore the Blog

Open the challenge URL in your browser.

You'll see a blog with several articles.

Look for an article tagged:

```text
#API Documentation
```

Click it.

It takes you to:

```text
/api-docs
```

This is **Swagger UI** — an interface that documents the application's available API endpoints.

Think of it as a **menu of APIs** that the server exposes. 📋

---

# 🔑 Step 2 — Find the Suspicious Endpoint

Scroll through the API documentation.

You'll find a section called:

```text
Diagnosing
```

Inside it is an endpoint:

```http
GET /heapdump
```

> 🚨 **This endpoint is suspicious.**

A heap-dump endpoint is generally intended for debugging or diagnostics and should not normally be exposed publicly.

If accessible without authentication, it can potentially expose sensitive information stored in application memory.

---

# 💥 Step 3 — Download the Heap Dump

You can access the endpoint directly through your browser:

```text
http://YOUR-CHALLENGE-URL:PORT/heapdump
```

The server should return a heap snapshot file similar to:

```text
heapdump-1789389564414.heapsnapshot
```

### 💻 PowerShell Method

You can also download it using PowerShell:

```powershell
Invoke-WebRequest -Uri "http://YOUR-CHALLENGE-URL:PORT/heapdump" -OutFile "heapdump.heapsnapshot"
```

The file will be saved as:

```text
heapdump.heapsnapshot
```

---

# 🏆 Step 4 — Find the Flag

The heap dump can be several megabytes in size, so manually reading it isn't practical.

Instead, search the file for the flag format.

### 🔍 PowerShell

```powershell
Select-String -Path ".\heapdump.heapsnapshot" -Pattern "picoCTF\{.*?\}" -AllMatches |
ForEach-Object { $_.Matches.Value }
```

### 🧩 Command Breakdown

| Command / Option   | Meaning                                     |
| ------------------ | ------------------------------------------- |
| `Select-String`    | Searches for text inside files              |
| `-Path`            | Specifies the heap dump file                |
| `-Pattern`         | Defines the text pattern to search for      |
| `picoCTF\{.*?\}`   | Matches text that looks like `picoCTF{...}` |
| `-AllMatches`      | Finds all matching occurrences              |
| `ForEach-Object`   | Processes each matching result              |
| `$_.Matches.Value` | Prints only the matched flag                |

If the flag is stored as plain text inside the heap dump, the command should reveal it.

---

# 🚩 Flag

```text
picoCTF{...}
```

> Replace `picoCTF{...}` with the actual flag obtained from your challenge instance.

